A program that calls `std.io:print` is asking for something no grammar rule can check: a console that exists on whatever this compiles to. `realize` is the solver for that question — and as of this week it is two modules in `koru_std`, not a koru-os command with a solver trapped inside it.

## The idiom already ships four times

`[comptime|norun]` marks a tor nobody calls: the call site is *data in the AST*, harvested after the parse by whoever asks. The family now counts four, differing only in disposition — what the declared data is *for*:

| module | tors | payload | consumer | disposition |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `std/build` | `requires`, `command`, `step`, `variants` | `Source` | build.zig generation | assembly spec — how the backend build gets assembled |
| `std/invariants` | `inferred`, `aspirational` | `Source` (prose) | `koruc <file> invariants` — a listing | rules declared, never enforced — addressed to a reader |
| `std/spec` | `spec`, plus `sites()` harvest | `Source` | any consumer | typed vocabulary — the contract surface |
| `std/realize` | `row`, `arm` | `iface: Expression` arg + `Source` (JSON body) | a solver — today `koruc <file> realize` | formal data, **decided** at configure |

`std/spec` is the base of the stack: the contract vocabulary — `FieldValue`, `NamedField`, `DispatchResult`, `DispatchFn`, the obligation and branch descriptors (`ScopeEvent`, `ScopeDescriptor`, `InputFieldSpec`) — moved wholesale out of `koru_runtime` and `koru_interpreter`, which now alias it. `runtime.specs` survives as a re-export shim so `koru_std.koru_runtime.ScopeEvent` keeps resolving; the types' home moved, the spelling didn't have to.

